2. Task 11 – “Who last modified the above document?” (.docx Analysis)
File: Salary_Lockheed_Martin_job_opportunities_confidential.docx (Lockheed Martin job-lure document).

Methods Attempted:  

Initial strings + grep (insufficient – metadata is inside compressed XML).  

Advanced extraction targeting OOXML structure.

CLI Commands Executed:


# Method 1: Direct extraction (recommended)

unzip -p Salary_Lockheed_Martin_job_opportunities_confidential.docx docProps/core.xml | grep -oP '(?<=<cp:lastModifiedBy>)[^<]+'

  

# Method 2: exiftool (cleanest output)

exiftool Salary_Lockheed_Martin_job_opportunities_confidential.docx | grep "Last Modified By"

Finding: Challenger (confirmed via both methods and Windows Properties dialog screenshot below).

“image.png” could not be found.
 

(Screenshot of “Details” tab showing “Last saved by: Challenger”)

Forensic Insight: The forged metadata was intentionally set by the attacker to increase credibility of the job-lure document – classic Lazarus social-engineering TTP.

3. Task 12 – “17.dotm” Analysis (Suspicious Folder Creation)
File: 17.dotm (malicious Word template containing VBA macro).

Methods Attempted:  

strings + grep on raw file (failed due to compressed VBA streams).  

Full VBA project extraction and de-obfuscation.

CLI Commands Executed:


# Extract VBA binary

unzip -p 17.dotm word/vbaProject.bin > vba.bin

  

# Hunt for filesystem operations

strings vba.bin | grep -iE "MkDir|CreateFolder|AppData|Roaming|Microsoft|STARTUP"

  

# Alternative: Full extraction + module inspection

unzip 17.dotm

strings word/_vbaProject.bin | grep -i "directory"

Finding: The macro creates a suspicious persistence directory immediately after <USER> (exact path extracted as AppData\Roaming\Microsoft\Word\STARTUP – submitted per placeholder format).

Forensic Insight: Aligns with DRATzarus loader behavior (T1137 Office Application Startup).

